<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-make-turkish-ezme">How to Make Turkish Ezme?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Making this authentic recipe is surprisingly simple, and it all starts with fresh, high-quality ingredients. This Turkish dip gets its signature texture from chopping everything very finely by hand, which gives the salad its unique, crushed consistency. Here is how I make it in my kitchen:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dipping-sauce.jpg" alt="Person chopping onions." class="wp-image-83036" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dipping-sauce.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dipping-sauce-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dipping-sauce-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dipping-sauce-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dipping-sauce-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dipping-sauce.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Mince and Soften the Aromatics:</strong> On a large cutting board, finely chop the onion and garlic. Sprinkle them with kosher salt and gently knead the mixture with your hands.<br>Adding the salt not only helps soften the raw onions but also mellow their sharpness. Transfer the mixture to a medium-size mixing bowl.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dip.jpg" alt="A collage of images showing of the cutting tomatoes and peppers." class="wp-image-83037" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dip.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dip-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dip-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dip-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dip-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-dip.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Add the Fresh Vegetables:</strong>&nbsp;Using a sharp knife, carefully remove the seeds from the tomatoes and chop them finely. If you&rsquo;d rather not fuss with seeding, chop them and give them a quick press through a fine mesh sieve to remove the extra liquid.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Next, remove the seeds from your peppers and chop them as finely as possible. When cutting peppers, I highly recommend wearing gloves if you have them. The oils can cause a burning sensation on your hands and sting <em>really</em> badly if you accidentally touch your eyes.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Transfer the tomatoes and the peppers to the bowl. Some cooks use a food processor for convenience, but I find it&rsquo;s easy to over-process and end up with more of a puree of vegetables. I think <strong>hand chopping is worth the effort</strong>, especially if you are looking for the most authentic version of this Turkish red sauce.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-Ezme-salad-prep.jpg" alt="Person mixing the ingredients for ezme in a bowl." class="wp-image-83038" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-Ezme-salad-prep.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-Ezme-salad-prep-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-Ezme-salad-prep-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-Ezme-salad-prep-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-Ezme-salad-prep-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Turkish-Ezme-salad-prep.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 3 &ndash; Mix in the Flavorings:</strong> Stir in the red pepper paste (or tomato paste), lemon juice, chopped parsley, sumac, and Urfa chili flakes (if using).</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 4 &ndash; Thoroughly Combine: </strong>Using a large spoon, gently mix everything together until all the ingredients are well incorporated and evenly coated. Don&rsquo;t rush this step, as ezme should feel like one unified mix, not separate vegetables.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 5 &ndash; Finish and Serve:</strong> You can serve it straight from the bowl, but if you want to do it the way we do it in Turkey, then transfer it to a small to medium-sized oval plate. Drizzle with pomegranate molasses or olive oil (if using) and serve right away, ideally with some warm pita or lavash on the side.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-make-ahead-and-storage-tips">Make Ahead and Storage Tips</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Make Ahead:</strong> This Turkish dip actually tastes even better after it has rested. I often make it a few hours in advance and let it chill in the fridge so the flavors can deepen and meld.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Keep in mind that the juices will settle as they sit, so you may have to drain them before serving.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Storage: </strong>Leftover ezme keeps well for 2-3 days when stored in an airtight container in the refrigerator. Just be sure to give it a good stir before serving, and again, drain the juices as needed.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Freezing: </strong>This dish is best when enjoyed fresh, so I do not recommend freezing it as it will lose its signature texture when thawed.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-expert-tips-from-my-turkish-kitchen">Expert Tips from My Turkish Kitchen</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Use a fine mince when chopping by hand: </strong>The secret to ezme&rsquo;s classic texture is how finely everything is chopped. You don&rsquo;t want big chunks in there. Take your time when chopping; it is part of what makes this recipe so good.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Dealing with excess juice:</strong> Earlier, I mentioned removing the seeds from the tomatoes to get the authentic texture that ezme is known for. But if you don&rsquo;t mind some extra juice, that&rsquo;s perfectly fine too. It will still taste fresh and delicious.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Using a food processor:</strong> My mom would probably turn in her grave if she saw me recommending a food processor when making ezme. Traditionally, everything is chopped by hand, and I still believe that&rsquo;s the best way to get the right texture. That said, I know it can be a real time-saver, especially if you&rsquo;re making a big batch.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you do decide to use one, here&rsquo;s what I recommend:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Start with the harder vegetables (onions, garlic, peppers) and chop them finely before adding the softer tomatoes.</li>



<li>Once you add the tomatoes, use short pulses instead of running the machine continuously. This helps you avoid ending up with a puree and keeps that slightly crushed consistency that makes ezme special.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Make it your own: </strong>Once you&rsquo;ve made the basic recipe I shared below, try creating your version by adding a bit more heat (there is a reason people ask for <em>&lsquo;acili ezme&rsquo;</em>, the spicy version of this dish) or incorporating some of the optional ingredients I mentioned above.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-serve-turkish-ezme-the-ultimate-kebab-side-dish">How to Serve Turkish Ezme (The Ultimate Kebab Side Dish)</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ezme is one of those dishes that goes with almost everything. While it&rsquo;s a classic alongside kebabs, its fresh, tangy flavor also makes it a great match for other Mediterranean favorites, from grilled meats to mezze platters.</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>With Kebabs or Grilled Meats: </strong>Ezme is the ultimate side to dishes like <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/adana-kebab/">Adana Kebab</a> or <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/chicken-kabobs-in-oven/">Chicken Kebab</a>. Its acidity and spice help balance the richness of the meat, which is exactly why it&rsquo;s such a common pairing in Turkey.</li>



<li><strong>Part of a Meze Platter:</strong> Add it to a Mediterranean-inspired mezze platter alongside <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/homemade-mediterranean-hummus/">hummus</a>, <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/baba-ganoush-recipe/">baba ganoush</a>, and <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/muhammara-recipe/">muhammara</a>. Don&rsquo;t forget the <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/lavash-bread-recipe/">lavash</a>, Turkish pide bread, or <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/homemade-pita-bread-recipe/">pita bread</a> (or pita chips) for scooping! This dish is meant for sharing.</li>



<li><strong>As a Spread or Sandwich Topping:</strong> Use leftover ezme as a spread for sandwiches or wraps the next day. It&rsquo;s especially good on my <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/lamb-burgers/">Grilled Lamb Burgers</a>, adding a bright, tangy kick that makes them even more special.</li>
</ul>
</div>



